<%# Keeps the focus ring keyboard-only.

    On Windows/Linux a mouse click focuses a <button> and it KEEPS focus; this app binds
    global keyboard shortcuts, so the next keypress re-evaluates :focus-visible and rings
    a control clicked ten seconds ago. (macOS does not focus buttons on click, which is
    why this only ever showed up in Edge.)

    Suppressing `pointerdown`'s default stops a pointer taking focus. Per the Pointer
    Events spec it does NOT suppress `click`, so every handler still runs, and keyboard
    activation fires no pointer event at all — Tab users keep the ring.

    Scoped to button/[role="button"]: the scrollbar thumb is a <div> dragged via
    @mousedown and must keep its default. Delegated on `document` so it survives the
    fetch()+innerHTML and fetch()+replaceWith() swaps that rebuild the controls. %>
<script>
  document.addEventListener('pointerdown', function (e) {
    if (e.target instanceof Element && e.target.closest('button, [role="button"]')) {
      e.preventDefault();
    }
  });
</script>
